8559"} "26 from 13 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This event featured a very strong and potentially close field. The New Zealand pair of " [:a {:href "/athletes/131317"} "Grace Prendergast"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/131320"} "Kerri Gowler"] " were considered the top favourite, being 2019 World Champions and holders of the world best time. Two other teams to watch, based on their performance at the 2019 Worlds, were Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/140642"} "Annabelle McIntyre"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/129043"} "Jessica Morrison"] ", and Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/129490"} "Caileigh Filmer"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/141417"} "Hillary Janssens"] ", respective silver and bronze medallists at that event. Another pair to be considered possible medal threats, if more recent success were any measure, were Russia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/146167"} "Vasilisa Stepanova"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/146170"} "Yelena Oryabinskaya"] ", victors in the final Olympic Qualification Regatta, and Great Britain featured 2016 Olympic champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/124338"} "Helen Glover"] " being paired with new teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/130273"} "Polly Swann"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "As expected, the favourites, with the exception of Australia, who faltered in the semi-finals, went through, with Greece and Spain filling out the finals field. This raised expectations for an exciting race, and the rowers did not disappoint. Canada broke out ahead at the start and at the midway point still held a narrow lead over New Zealand. The two ran close for a spell with New Zealand gradually edging ahead. Then the Russian team found another gear to overtake Canada and make a push at the leaders, but New Zealand held on for the gold, with the Russians taking silver, and Canada the bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {